package Vixen.TextureGraph.Shaders

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// Doc 48 § 4.1's noise: one kernel with five bases — value · gradient · worley · white · gaussian —
// under octaves, lacunarity, gain and a seed, wrapping wherever the plan does. Worley also answers with F1, F2 and a cell index,
// "which is what a splatter wants and what saves a flood fill downstream", and with a **distance
// metric**, which is what a cell's shape is (#1101).
//
// ## ⚠ The basis is a uniform, and § 4.1 asked for a `[Permutation]`
//
// It cannot be one. `TexturePlanEvaluator.VariantFor` compiles `EffectKey.Of(kernel)` with no
// defines and caches the result on `(kernel, output format)` — there is no plumbing anywhere in the
// evaluator M1 built through which a plan could name a permutation value, so a `[Permutation]` here
// would silently take its `.rvn` default in every op for ever. That is the repository's registered
// trap (`PermutationKeys`) arriving from the other side: not a value missing from the key list, but
// no key list at all. A uniform is also what `Shaders/README.md` already argues for the blend mode,
// and the argument is stronger here — four bases times three output formats is twelve modules for a
// branch that every invocation in a dispatch takes the same way.
//
// ## ⚠ The hash is `Raven/Library/Core/Random.rvn`'s, called — and this file carried a copy of it
//
// `Random.Hash`, `Random.Combine` and `Random.ToFloat01` used to be written out here under a header
// saying they could not be reached, because "a texture-graph kernel is compiled alone —
// `RavenEffectCompiler.FromSources` is given one tree and no `.rvnlib`". Half of that was true and
// the half naming a cause was not. `FromSources` takes a *set* of texts and makes them one
// compilation; the evaluator was passing one text. `TextureKernelPrelude` passes the library beside
// every kernel now, so the import resolves and there is one hash rather than five — #635.
//
// ⚠ **`ComputeColor.ValueNoise` and `ComputeColor.FractalNoise` are still not called**, and that is
// a choice rather than a leftover: they are very nearly `ValueAt` and `Fractal` and *very nearly* is
// the problem. Neither takes a period, so neither can tile, which is what this kernel's `tiling`
// needs.
//
// ⚠ **That decision was taken on 2026-09-08 and the answer is no** (#1077). The blast radius is one
// node each — `ProceduralNodes.NoiseNode` and `FractalNoiseNode` are the only production callers of
// `ComputeColor.ValueNoise` and `FractalNoise` in the tree — so the refusal is not about cost. It is
// that a wrapping lattice needs a whole number of cells, and the paragraph below says this kernel
// *rounds* to one rather than refusing, because a bake is offline and a seam is worse than a rounded
// frequency. A material sampling per fragment has no such licence, so a period on the library's pair
// would be a parameter that is silently wrong at every non-integer scale in the one place it would
// be exposed.
//
// ## The seed
//
// `seed` arrives as a float because `TexturePlanEvaluator` supplies it that way — twenty-four bits of
// `TexturePlan.SeedFor(op)`, hashed on the CPU where it is the same on every machine. What is needed
// of it here is only that two noise ops disagree, and twenty-four bits of a mixed seed is that.
//
// ## Tiling, and what it needs of the other parameters
//
// A tiling noise is a lattice with a whole number of cells, so along an axis that wraps every
// octave's frequency is **rounded** to one, and that rounding is what keeps it tiling at any `scale`
// and any `lacunarity`: `scale = 5.5` draws 6 cells rather than a seam. An axis that does not wrap
// keeps the exact frequency, so only a wrapping axis has a `Scale` that moves in steps.
//
// ## Resolution
//
// Everything here is a function of the normalised position, so a noise field is the same picture at
// every resolution — which is also why **white noise is per *cell* and not per texel.** A per-texel
// white noise is a different image at 1K and at 4K by construction, and doc 48 § D8 requires that it
// not be. An author who wants one texel per sample sets `scale` to the resolution and says so.

// ## What Patina #167 added: a lattice per axis, a shift, a first octave and a fold
//
// `scaleX` and `scaleY` multiply `scale` along one axis each, so a lattice can be eight cells across
// and sixty-four down — a streak, a grain, a fibre — without a `Transform 2D` that would need the
// stretch to be a whole number of repeats to keep tiling. Each axis rounds to its own whole period
// where it wraps, which is the same rule as before, applied twice.
//
// `offsetX` and `offsetY` shift the lattice by cells of octave zero along that axis — the cells `scale`
// counts, rounded where the axis wraps — and every octave moves the same distance in the picture. So a
// shift of half is half a cell whatever the scale, which is what a running bond or a hex row wants
// from a per-cell noise, a shift of `scale` is the whole image and the identity on an axis that wraps,
// and a shift that is a whole number of texels rolls the picture by exactly that many.
//
// `minOctave` drops the coarsest octaves: octave `minOctave` is the first one summed, at its own
// frequency and its own hash. So a two-octave sum is exactly the first octave plus `gain` times the
// one-octave sum that starts at octave one, normalised — `TextureNoiseFamilyDeviceTests` holds that
// identity rather than a picture.
//
// `profile` folds each octave before it is summed: `1 − |2v − 1|` is ridged — a crease where the
// plain value crosses a half — and `|2v − 1|` is its complement, a billow. Folding the sum instead
// would crease once, at one level, which is a contour line rather than a ridged field.

shader Noise {
    /// 0 value · 1 gradient · 2 worley · 3 white · 4 gaussian.
    var basis: int = 0

    /// 0 euclidean · 1 chebyshev · 2 manhattan. ⚠ Worley only — see `Reach`.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **A uniform and not a `[Permutation]`, for `basis`'s reason above and not by analogy with
    /// it**: there is no plumbing through which a plan could name a permutation value, so one here
    /// would take this default in every op for ever. Twelve modules become thirty-six if both are
    /// permutations, for two branches every invocation in a dispatch takes the same way.
    var metric: int = 0

    /// How many cells across the image at the first octave.
    var scale: float = 8f

    /// A multiplier on `scale` along x.
    var scaleX: float = 1f

    /// A multiplier on `scale` along y.
    var scaleY: float = 1f

    /// A shift of the lattice along x, in cells of octave zero.
    var offsetX: float = 0f

    /// A shift of the lattice along y, in cells of octave zero.
    var offsetY: float = 0f

    /// The first octave summed. The ones below it are skipped, at their frequencies and their hashes.
    var minOctave: int = 0

    /// 0 plain · 1 ridged · 2 billow — how each octave is folded before it is summed.
    var profile: int = 0

    /// How many octaves are summed. Worley sums F1 and F2 and keeps the first octave's index.
    var octaves: int = 1

    /// The frequency multiplier between octaves. 2 is the usual choice.
    var lacunarity: float = 2f

    /// The amplitude multiplier between octaves. 0.5 is the usual choice.
    var gain: float = 0.5f

    /// Non-zero when the lattice wraps across the image, so the picture is seamless repeated. The
    /// plan's, as `seed` is: see `Math.Address`.
    var wrapX: int = 0

    /// The same down the image.
    var wrapY: int = 0

    /// The op's own seed, already mixed. See `TexturePlan.SeedFor`.
    var seed: float = 0f

    /// Another draw of the same noise, as a whole number the author chooses: nought is the op's own seed, so every
    /// picture drawn before this existed is unchanged, and every other number is a different pattern of the same
    /// character. Patina #120's seed on a generator.
    var variation: float = 0f

    [Format("rgba16f")] var target: RWTexture2D<float4>

    const val TwoPi: float = 6.2831853f

    /// 2D Perlin peaks at √2 / 2, so this is what maps its range onto 0..1.
    const val PerlinScale: float = 0.70710678f

    /// The most octaves a plan can ask for. A ceiling rather than a runtime bound, for `Blur`'s
    /// reason: an octave count arriving as a NaN would be a loop no invocation leaves.
    const val MaxOctaves: int = 8

    /// The latest octave the sum may start at, so the finest one summed is octave fourteen.
    const val MaxMinOctave: int = 7

    /// The spread of a gaussian lattice value about a half: three of them reach either end, so the
    /// clamp at the end of `Main` cuts about one value in four hundred.
    const val GaussianSigma: float = 0.16666667f

    /// A sentinel strictly above every metric's bound on a 3×3 search, so the first candidate always
    /// replaces it.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Not a normalisation constant and it used to read as one.** A candidate's offset has each
    /// component strictly inside ±2 cells, so the bound is 2√2 under euclidean, 2 under chebyshev
    /// and **4 under manhattan** — this was 4, which is exactly manhattan's bound and would have left
    /// `reach < f1` resting on the jitter never reaching one. What normalises the answer is
    /// `Normalisation`, which is per metric.
    const val WorleyFar: float = 8f

    /// The seed as the integer the hash wants, with the author's variation folded in when there is one.
    func Seeded(): uint {
        val own = uint(int(max(seed, 0f)))

        if (variation < 0.5f) {
            return own
        }

        return Random.Combine(own, uint(int(variation + 0.5f)))
    }

    /// One lattice coordinate, wrapped when the noise tiles.
    func Fold(value: int, period: int, wrap: int): int {
        if (wrap == 0) {
            return value
        }

        if (period <= 0) {
            return value
        }

        // Twice, because `%` follows the sign of the dividend in both of Raven's backends and a cell
        // left of the origin would otherwise fold to a negative index — which hashes perfectly well
        // and is a different cell from the one on the far side, so the seam comes back.
        return ((value % period) + period) % period
    }

    /// The hash of one cell, under the seed, the octave and the tiling period.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The octave is in the hash, and it was not.** Each octave reads the same integers at a
    /// higher frequency, so at a lacunarity of two the second octave's top-left quarter was the first
    /// octave shrunk: they correlated at 1.000, the image held copies of itself toward the corner, and
    /// the value at `uv = 0` was the same at any octave count. Added to the seed so that the first
    /// octave, and so a single-octave noise and every Worley, is the picture it always was.
    ///
    /// ⚠ A block body rather than a `=>` broken over two lines. A newline ends a statement in Raven,
    /// so the continuation would have been a second statement and the first would have had no
    /// expression at all — which `RVN1001` does catch here, and does not catch when the continuation
    /// begins with an operator.
    func CellHash(x: int, y: int, octave: int, period: int2): uint {
        val cell = Random.Combine(uint(Fold(x, period.x, wrapX)), uint(Fold(y, period.y, wrapY)))

        return Random.Combine(cell, Seeded() + Random.Hash(uint(octave)))
    }

    /// How many cells across the image the first octave has along one axis: `scale` times that
    /// axis's multiplier.
    func Cells(multiplier: float): float => scale * max(multiplier, 0f)

    /// The whole number of cells one octave has along an axis that wraps.
    func Period(cells: float, frequency: float): int => int(round(max(cells, 1f) * frequency))

    /// One octave's period per axis.
    func Periods(frequency: float): int2 => int2(Period(Cells(scaleX), frequency), Period(Cells(scaleY), frequency))

    /// How many cells one unit of `uv` crosses along an axis: the period where it wraps.
    func Stride(cells: float, frequency: float, period: int, wrap: int): float {
        if (wrap == 0) {
            return max(cells, 0.00001f) * frequency
        }

        return float(max(period, 1))
    }

    /// The offset in units of the image: cells of octave zero, which is one over its stride.
    func Shift(): float2 {
        val zero = Periods(1f)
        val acrossX = Stride(Cells(scaleX), 1f, zero.x, wrapX)
        val acrossY = Stride(Cells(scaleY), 1f, zero.y, wrapY)

        return float2(offsetX / acrossX, offsetY / acrossY)
    }

    /// Where `uv` lands on one octave's lattice: shifted by the offset, then scaled per axis.
    func Lattice(uv: float2, frequency: float, period: int2): float2 {
        val strideX = Stride(Cells(scaleX), frequency, period.x, wrapX)
        val strideY = Stride(Cells(scaleY), frequency, period.y, wrapY)

        return (uv + Shift()) * float2(strideX, strideY)
    }

    /// One octave's value folded by `profile`.
    func Folded(value: float): float {
        if (profile == 1) {
            return 1f - abs(2f * value - 1f)
        }

        if (profile == 2) {
            return abs(2f * value - 1f)
        }

        return value
    }

    /// The frequency of the first octave summed: the lacunarity raised to `minOctave`, by repeated
    /// multiplication so that octave zero's is exactly one.
    func FirstFrequency(): float {
        var frequency = 1f
        val skipped = clamp(minOctave, 0, MaxMinOctave)

        for (octave in 0 .. MaxMinOctave - 1) {
            if (octave >= skipped) {
                break
            }

            frequency = frequency * max(lacunarity, 1f)
        }

        return frequency
    }

    /// Smoothed value noise: a hash per lattice corner, bilinear under a smoothstep.
    func ValueAt(position: float2, octave: int, period: int2): float {
        val cell = floor(position)
        val local = position - cell
        val weights = local * local * (3f - 2f * local)
        val x = int(cell.x)
        val y = int(cell.y)

        val c00 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x, y, octave, period))
        val c10 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x + 1, y, octave, period))
        val c01 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x, y + 1, octave, period))
        val c11 = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x + 1, y + 1, octave, period))

        return lerp(lerp(c00, c10, weights.x), lerp(c01, c11, weights.x), weights.y)
    }

    /// One corner's gradient dotted with the offset to it.
    func GradientDot(x: int, y: int, octave: int, period: int2, delta: float2): float {
        val angle = Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(x, y, octave, period)) * TwoPi

        return delta.x * cos(angle) + delta.y * sin(angle)
    }

    /// Gradient (Perlin) noise, remapped from ±√2/2 onto 0..1.
    func GradientAt(position: float2, octave: int, period: int2): float {
        val cell = floor(position)
        val local = position - cell
        val fade = local * local * local * (local * (local * 6f - 15f) + 10f)
        val x = int(cell.x)
        val y = int(cell.y)

        val g00 = GradientDot(x, y, octave, period, local)
        val g10 = GradientDot(x + 1, y, octave, period, local - float2(1f, 0f))
        val g01 = GradientDot(x, y + 1, octave, period, local - float2(0f, 1f))
        val g11 = GradientDot(x + 1, y + 1, octave, period, local - float2(1f, 1f))

        val under = lerp(g00, g10, fade.x)
        val over = lerp(g01, g11, fade.x)

        return lerp(under, over, fade.y) * PerlinScale + 0.5f
    }

    /// One uncorrelated value per cell — value noise without the interpolation.
    func WhiteAt(position: float2, octave: int, period: int2): float {
        val cell = floor(position)

        return Random.ToFloat01(CellHash(int(cell.x), int(cell.y), octave, period))
    }

    /// One lattice corner drawn from a normal distribution about a half, by Box–Muller over two
    /// hashes of the cell.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The first uniform is kept off zero**, because `log(0)` is an infinity, and one corner in
    /// four billion would otherwise be a NaN that the final `saturate` is not obliged to turn into
    /// anything in particular.
    func GaussianCorner(x: int, y: int, octave: int, period: int2): float {
        val hash = CellHash(x, y, octave, period)
        val first = max(Random.ToFloat01(hash), 0.0000001f)
        val second = Random.ToFloat01(Random.Hash(hash ^ 0x5BD1E995u))

        return 0.5f + GaussianSigma * sqrt(-2f * log(first)) * cos(TwoPi * second)
    }

    /// Value noise over a gaussian lattice: the same smoothstep between four corners, so the field is
    /// as smooth as value noise and its histogram is a bell rather than a plateau.
    func GaussianAt(position: float2, octave: int, period: int2): float {
        val cell = floor(position)
        val local = position - cell
        val weights = local * local * (3f - 2f * local)
        val x = int(cell.x)
        val y = int(cell.y)

        val c00 = GaussianCorner(x, y, octave, period)
        val c10 = GaussianCorner(x + 1, y, octave, period)
        val c01 = GaussianCorner(x, y + 1, octave, period)
        val c11 = GaussianCorner(x + 1, y + 1, octave, period)

        return lerp(lerp(c00, c10, weights.x), lerp(c01, c11, weights.x), weights.y)
    }

    /// Whichever basis a fractal octave is made of.
    func Octave(position: float2, octave: int, period: int2): float {
        if (basis == 1) {
            return GradientAt(position, octave, period)
        }

        if (basis == 3) {
            return WhiteAt(position, octave, period)
        }

        if (basis == 4) {
            return GaussianAt(position, octave, period)
        }

        return ValueAt(position, octave, period)
    }

    /// Octaves at multiplying frequency and multiplying amplitude, normalised back to 0..1.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Normalised by the sum of the amplitudes rather than by a constant**, so a gain that is not
    /// 0.5 still fills the range — an octave stack divided by two per level is a field that never
    /// reaches white and reads as a washed-out noise nobody can find the cause of.
    func Fractal(uv: float2): float {
        var total = 0f
        var amplitude = 1f
        var normalisation = 0f
        var frequency = FirstFrequency()
        val wanted = clamp(octaves, 1, MaxOctaves)
        val first = clamp(minOctave, 0, MaxMinOctave)

        for (summed in 0 .. MaxOctaves - 1) {
            if (summed >= wanted) {
                break
            }

            val period = Periods(frequency)
            val value = Octave(Lattice(uv, frequency, period), first + summed, period)

            total = total + Folded(value) * amplitude
            normalisation = normalisation + amplitude
            frequency = frequency * max(lacunarity, 1f)
            amplitude = amplitude * max(gain, 0f)
        }

        return total / max(normalisation, 0.000001f)
    }

    /// How far a feature point is, in cells, under the chosen metric.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The metric is what a cell's *shape* is**, and it was euclidean and nothing else until
    /// [#1101](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1101): chebyshev gives square cells and
    /// manhattan gives diamonds, and those two are most of what a cellular pattern library is for —
    /// tile, cracked mud, snake skin, hex-ish plate. Round cells were not a design decision, they
    /// were the only thing `length` could say.
    func Reach(delta: float2): float {
        if (metric == 1) {
            return max(abs(delta.x), abs(delta.y))
        }

        if (metric == 2) {
            return abs(delta.x) + abs(delta.y)
        }

        return length(delta)
    }

    /// What F1 and F2 are divided by, so that neither clips.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Derived from the metric's own bound on this search, not tuned.** Euclidean has always
    /// reported "in units of two cells" — a half — against a bound of 2√2, which is 1/√2 of its
    /// headroom. Chebyshev's bound is 2 and manhattan's is 4, so keeping the same fraction of
    /// headroom gives `0.5 · 2√2 / 2` and `0.5 · 2√2 / 4`. ⚠ **Reusing the half would have clipped
    /// manhattan** on exactly the channel a cell border is computed from: F2 would read as a wide
    /// plateau of white and `F2 − F1` would vanish along with it. Euclidean's number is written as
    /// the literal it always was, so every picture already baked is unchanged to the bit.
    func Normalisation(): float {
        if (metric == 1) {
            return 0.70710678f
        }

        if (metric == 2) {
            return 0.35355339f
        }

        return 0.5f
    }

    /// F1, F2 and a cell index from one octave's lattice, the two distances already normalised.
    func WorleyOctave(uv: float2, frequency: float, octave: int): float3 {
        val period = Periods(frequency)
        val position = Lattice(uv, frequency, period)
        val origin = floor(position)

        var f1 = WorleyFar
        var f2 = WorleyFar
        var index = 0f

        for (row in -1 .. 1) {
            for (column in -1 .. 1) {
                val x = int(origin.x) + column
                val y = int(origin.y) + row
                val hash = CellHash(x, y, octave, period)
                val jitter = float2(Random.ToFloat01(hash), Random.ToFloat01(Random.Hash(hash ^ 0x68BC21EBu)))
                val point = float2(origin.x + float(column), origin.y + float(row)) + jitter
                val reach = Reach(point - position)

                if (reach < f1) {
                    f2 = f1
                    f1 = reach
                    index = Random.ToFloat01(Random.Hash(hash ^ 0x1B873593u))
                } else {
                    if (reach < f2) {
                        f2 = reach
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // ⚠ **Reported in units of two cells under euclidean, so that neither distance clips.** A
        // 3×3 search's second-nearest point is regularly further than one cell away, and a
        // `saturate` at one cell would flatten exactly the channel a splatter uses to find a cell's
        // border — F2 would read as a wide plateau of white and the difference F2 − F1, which is the
        // border, would vanish along with it. ⚠ The other two metrics do **not** share that scale,
        // which is why `Normalisation` exists rather than a literal here: the same picture measured
        // with manhattan is up to √2 further away, and the half that gives euclidean its headroom
        // is a clip there.
        val normalisation = Normalisation()

        return float3(saturate(f1 * normalisation), saturate(f2 * normalisation), index)
    }

    /// F1 and F2 summed over octaves, and the cell index of the first octave summed.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Worley ignored `octaves` until Patina #167, and the reason it gave still holds for one
    /// channel of three.** F1 and F2 summed over octaves are grey fields like any other fractal sum —
    /// fractal cells, cracked mud at two scales — but the cell index of a sum of lattices names
    /// nothing, so blue is the first summed octave's and the sum leaves it alone. A one-octave Worley
    /// is the picture it always was, because octave zero's hash is the one the kernel always used.
    func Worley(uv: float2): float3 {
        var distances = float2(0f, 0f)
        var amplitude = 1f
        var normalisation = 0f
        var frequency = FirstFrequency()
        var index = 0f
        val wanted = clamp(octaves, 1, MaxOctaves)
        val first = clamp(minOctave, 0, MaxMinOctave)

        for (summed in 0 .. MaxOctaves - 1) {
            if (summed >= wanted) {
                break
            }

            val cells = WorleyOctave(uv, frequency, first + summed)

            if (summed == 0) {
                index = cells.z
            }

            distances = distances + float2(Folded(cells.x), Folded(cells.y)) * amplitude
            normalisation = normalisation + amplitude
            frequency = frequency * max(lacunarity, 1f)
            amplitude = amplitude * max(gain, 0f)
        }

        val mean = distances / max(normalisation, 0.000001f)

        return float3(mean.x, mean.y, index)
    }

    [ComputeShader(8, 8, 1)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3) {
        val coord = int2(int(id.x), int(id.y))
        val size = target.GetDimensions()

        if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y) {
            return
        }

        val uv = float2((float(coord.x) + 0.5f) / float(size.x), (float(coord.y) + 0.5f) / float(size.y))

        // ⚠ Worley is the one basis whose output is not grey, and the channels are named here rather
        // than left to a reader: red is F1, green is F2, blue is the cell index. Doc 48 § 4.1 asks for
        // "grey + cell id" and this is it — a downstream `Channel Shuffle` is what picks one out.
        if (basis == 2) {
            val cells = Worley(uv)

            target.Store(coord, float4(cells.x, cells.y, cells.z, 1f))

            return
        }

        val value = saturate(Fractal(uv))

        target.Store(coord, float4(value, value, value, 1f))
    }
}
